      "answer": "Based on the lecture material provided, here is the explanation of arithmetic intensity and how memory bandwidth limits performance.\n\n### 1. What is Arithmetic Intensity?\n\nArithmetic intensity is defined as the ratio of floating-point operations (FLOPs) to the number of bytes moved from memory. It quantifies how much computation is performed for each byte of data transferred.\n\nThe formula provided in the lecture is:\n$$ \\text{Arithmetic Intensity} = \\frac{\\text{FLOPs}}{\\text{Bytes moved from memory}} $$\n\nThis metric is central to the **Roofline Model**, which helps determine whether a program is **compute-bound** or **memory-bound**. [S1][S6]\n\n### 2. How Memory Bandwidth Limits Performance\n\nMemory bandwidth limits performance when a program is **memory-bound**. In this scenario, the performance is capped by the product of the memory bandwidth and the arithmetic intensity.\n\n*   **The Limit:** If a program has low arithmetic intensity, it cannot utilize the full computational power of the hardware because it is waiting for data to be moved from memory. The lecture states that in a memory-bound case, performance is \"limited by bandwidth × intensity.\" [S1][S6]\n*   **The \"Memory Wall\":** The lecture refers to this limitation as the \"memory wall,\" which describes how much computation can be performed for each byte moved. [S1][S6]\n*   **Real-world Ceilings:** Even if a program is not strictly memory-bound (i.e., it is compute-bound), real performance ceilings can still fall due to poor locality or pipeline utilization, which are related to how efficiently data is accessed. [S1][S6]\n\n### Summary of the Roofline Model Logic\n\nThe Roofline Model uses arithmetic intensity to distinguish between two regimes:\n1.  **Memory-Bound:** Performance is limited by memory bandwidth. The ceiling is determined by `Bandwidth × Arithmetic Intensity`. [S1][S6]\n2.  **Compute-Bound:** Performance is limited by the peak computational throughput (FLOPs). However, achieving this peak still requires effective vectorization, use of tensor cores, and good occupancy. [S1][S6]\n\n**Source Citations:**\n*   Definition of Arithmetic Intensity and Roofline Model: Page 67 [S1][S6]\n*   Context on Performance Models: Page 64 [S4]\n*   General Performance Limits (Load Imbalance, etc.): Page 65 [S5]",
